---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import Stage from "../../components/Stage.astro";
---
<Base title="Tabs: ARIA tabs from plain HTML" description="iVOLT tabs: anchor links and sections served as plain HTML, promoted to the ARIA tabs pattern by JavaScript with automatic or manual activation.">
  <article class="docs-prose" data-enriched>
    <h1>Tabs</h1>
    <p>The server sends anchor links and sections with headings, which work as in-page navigation without JavaScript. On <code>init</code> the component promotes them to the ARIA tabs pattern and hides inactive panels; <code>destroy</code> restores the served HTML.</p>
    <p class="docs-meta"><strong>CSS:</strong> <code>css/components/tabs.css</code>. <strong>JS:</strong> <code>@intervolutions/ivolt/tabs</code> or <code>auto.js</code>.</p>
    <Stage primary name="tabs/basic" title="Tabs and panels" />
    <Section title="Options" num={1}>
      <div class="docs-scroller">
        <table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Attribute</th><th>Default</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody>
          <tr><td><code>activation</code></td><td><code>data-iv-activation</code></td><td><code>automatic</code></td><td><code>automatic</code> selects on arrow-key focus; <code>manual</code> waits for Enter or Space.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>orientation</code></td><td><code>data-iv-orientation</code></td><td><code>horizontal</code></td><td><code>vertical</code> uses Up/Down and the <code>iv-tabs--vertical</code> layout.</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Methods and events" num={2} lead={`select(idOrElement), next(), prev(), activeTab, destroy(). iv:change (cancelable, detail.tab, panel, previousTab) then iv:changed.`}>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Keyboard" num={3} lead={`Left/Right (or Up/Down when vertical) move between tabs with wrap-around, Home and End jump to the first and last; Tab moves into the panel.`}>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Without JavaScript" num={4} lead={`All panels stay visible with their headings; the links scroll to each section. Essential content therefore never depends on the script.`}>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Common mistakes" num={5}>
      <ul><li>Putting <code>role="tab"</code> in the served HTML: without the script it announces a widget that does not work.</li><li>Tabs for sequential steps; use a form flow.</li></ul>
    </Section>
    <Section title="Customising" num={6}>
      <p>Locals: <code>--iv-tabs-indicator</code> (the colour of the line under the active tab) and <code>--iv-tabs-gap</code>. The vertical layout is a modifier, <code>iv-tabs--vertical</code>, paired with <code>data-iv-orientation="vertical"</code> so the arrow keys follow the layout.</p><pre tabindex="0"><code>.settings .iv-tabs &#123; --iv-tabs-indicator: var(--iv-color-accent); &#125;</code></pre>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Linking to a tab" num={7}>
      <p>Each panel keeps the id it was served with, and each tab is a link to it, so a URL such as <code>/pricing#p-yearly</code> is still a meaningful address: without the script it scrolls to that section, and the headings inside the panels (<code>iv-tabs__heading</code>) give every section a name either way.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Tabs or something else?" num={8}>
      <p>Tabs suit a few parallel views of the same thing — plans, settings groups, code in several languages — where the reader needs one at a time. With more than five or six tabs, or labels that do not fit on one line on a phone, use a list of sections or a <a href="/components/disclosure">disclosure</a> group instead. For steps that must be done in order, use the <a href="/components/stepper">stepper</a>.</p>
    </Section>
  </article>
</Base>
